WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.970
If someone asked you why you love the person you love, you would have a list ready: their good heart, their smile, the way they talk to you.

2
00:00:07.970 --> 00:00:14.980
But if tomorrow someone turns up with more of those very same qualities, are you supposed to transfer your love to them?

3
00:00:14.980 --> 00:00:21.030
If your answer is "no," then those qualities you listed are not the reason for your love — so what is?

4
00:00:29.230 --> 00:00:37.200
If someone asked you why you love the person you love, you would have a list ready: their good heart, their laugh, the particular way they talk to you.

5
00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:42.770
But suppose that tomorrow somebody turns up carrying more of every one of those same qualities.

6
00:00:42.770 --> 00:00:46.260
Are you then obliged to transfer your love over to them?

7
00:00:46.260 --> 00:00:51.350
If your answer is no, then those qualities you listed are not the reason for your love.

8
00:00:51.350 --> 00:00:53.400
So what is?

9
00:00:53.400 --> 00:01:04.410
At a dinner table one evening, after a long silence, someone turns to the person sitting across from them and says: I love you for your mind, for that strange sense of humour of yours,

10
00:01:04.410 --> 00:01:07.740
for that stubbornness nobody else can pull off.

11
00:01:07.740 --> 00:01:12.030
It sounds like one of the most beautiful sentences a person could hear.

12
00:01:12.030 --> 00:01:14.880
The other one smiles.

13
00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:21.730
But listen to that sentence again, this time taking it seriously, assuming it means precisely what it says.

14
00:01:21.730 --> 00:01:29.380
Because there, inside the compliment, there is a crack that takes about a second to notice and then never quite leaves your head again.

15
00:01:29.380 --> 00:01:34.790
If you love me for my intelligence, what happens when you meet someone more intelligent than I am?

16
00:01:34.790 --> 00:01:41.000
If you love me for my sense of humour, shouldn't the address of your love change the moment somebody funnier walks in?

17
00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:48.650
The logic is mercilessly clean: if you love a thing on account of a property, then you ought to love more the thing that carries more of that property.

18
00:01:48.650 --> 00:01:54.060
If you choose a fabric for its softness, you will prefer the softer one.

19
00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:59.470
If you rent a house for its view, you move out when a house with a better view comes on the market.

20
00:01:59.470 --> 00:02:08.560
In philosophy this is called the problem of replaceability, and it stands like a wall in front of almost every theory that has ever tried to explain love.

21
00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:10.610
Think of it this way.

22
00:02:10.610 --> 00:02:13.140
You go to a job interview.

23
00:02:13.140 --> 00:02:16.630
What is asked of you there is to enumerate your qualities.

24
00:02:16.630 --> 00:02:21.400
I learn quickly, I speak 3 languages, I work well with a team.

25
00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:25.370
Nobody in that room loves you; they are assessing you.

26
00:02:25.370 --> 00:02:32.380
And if the following day somebody walks through the same door carrying the same qualities with a little more of each, the job is theirs.

27
00:02:32.380 --> 00:02:34.910
Nobody calls that a betrayal.

28
00:02:34.910 --> 00:02:38.240
It is called hiring.

29
00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:47.010
Now here is the genuinely uncomfortable question: when we describe love as the sum of a person's properties, what separates it from a hiring process?

30
00:02:47.010 --> 00:02:51.940
Is the beloved merely the temporary carrier of the qualities they happen to hold?

31
00:02:51.940 --> 00:02:59.590
Is the appearance of those same qualities in another body, in a slightly brighter version, sufficient grounds for moving love over there?

32
00:02:59.590 --> 00:03:03.880
There is a thought experiment that tightens this to the point of pain.

33
00:03:03.880 --> 00:03:10.410
Suppose a duplicate of the person you love turned up, someone carrying every single one of their properties exactly.

34
00:03:10.410 --> 00:03:18.860
The same voice, the same humour, the same walk, the same impatience, the same childhood memories, the same fragility.

35
00:03:18.860 --> 00:03:22.350
Not one line on the list of qualities is different.

36
00:03:22.350 --> 00:03:29.040
If your love is truly a response given to that list, then you have no grounds whatsoever for choosing between the two.

37
00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:31.250
You may as well flip a coin.

38
00:03:31.250 --> 00:03:34.900
And yet almost nobody accepts this conclusion.

39
00:03:34.900 --> 00:03:40.630
Everyone, even when they cannot explain why, says: no, I want that one.

40
00:03:40.630 --> 00:03:46.680
And right there, in that stubbornness we cannot account for, our subject is standing.

41
00:03:46.680 --> 00:03:50.490
But suppose we go over to the other end of the dilemma.

42
00:03:50.490 --> 00:03:57.980
Suppose we reverse the answer and say: I love you for none of your qualities, I love you for no reason at all.

43
00:03:57.980 --> 00:04:01.310
It sounds more romantic, doesn't it.

44
00:04:01.310 --> 00:04:04.640
But take that seriously for a second as well.

45
00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:10.050
If I love you for no reason, then my love has nothing to do with any aspect of you.

46
00:04:10.050 --> 00:04:17.700
The kind of person you are, the things you care about, the way you treat people, contribute nothing whatsoever to your being loved.

47
00:04:17.700 --> 00:04:22.310
It could have been someone else; it could have been whoever came through the door first.

48
00:04:22.310 --> 00:04:29.640
My love says nothing about you at all, it says only something about me: I am the sort of person who attaches like this.

49
00:04:29.640 --> 00:04:36.010
At first glance this looks like unconditional love, but look inside it and it resembles a kind of indifference.

50
00:04:36.010 --> 00:04:39.980
What is taken for praise turns out to be a failure to see.

51
00:04:39.980 --> 00:04:43.470
And there is a third case, the quietest one.

52
00:04:43.470 --> 00:04:46.320
Properties do not stay put.

53
00:04:46.320 --> 00:04:54.770
A traffic accident, a diagnosis, a person who has lost their job coming home for 6 months with the light gone out of their eyes.

54
00:04:54.770 --> 00:05:02.100
The one you spent years describing as the funniest person in your life loses someone close to them and does not laugh for a year.

55
00:05:02.100 --> 00:05:07.350
Where the quality you listed as the reason for your love used to be, there is now a gap.

56
00:05:07.350 --> 00:05:12.920
If the grounds have been removed, must the thing that rested on those grounds be removed too?

57
00:05:12.920 --> 00:05:17.850
When the clause you signed the contract for disappears, the contract lapses.

58
00:05:17.850 --> 00:05:21.820
If love is also a contract, it ought to lapse.

59
00:05:21.820 --> 00:05:25.950
And yet people stay precisely during those periods.

60
00:05:25.950 --> 00:05:34.560
Someone sitting in a hospital corridor, holding the hand of a person who no longer recognises them, is doing something that fits into no table of logic.

61
00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:38.690
So what we have is a pair of jaws closing from both sides.

62
00:05:38.690 --> 00:05:46.020
If you love for the properties, then what you love is not the person but the qualities they carry; the person becomes replaceable.

63
00:05:46.020 --> 00:05:52.710
If you love independently of the properties, love becomes arbitrary and blind; the person disappears again.

64
00:05:52.710 --> 00:05:58.440
Whichever of the two doors you walk through, you cannot find the person you love anywhere in the room.

65
00:05:58.440 --> 00:06:02.410
This dilemma is not a dilemma of our own age.

66
00:06:02.410 --> 00:06:11.180
Roughly 2400 years ago, in a house in Athens, a group of men talking around a wine table ran straight into this same question.

67
00:06:11.180 --> 00:06:17.870
And one of them decided to open the first door all the way, to see where loving for the properties actually leads.

68
00:06:17.870 --> 00:06:23.280
Here is the scene: the year is 416 BC, in Athens.

69
00:06:23.280 --> 00:06:29.170
The young poet Agathon has won the tragedy competition and has gathered his friends to celebrate.

70
00:06:29.170 --> 00:06:38.100
Because the previous night's drinking went badly, they decide not to drink much this evening; instead they propose that each of them in turn make a speech about love.

71
00:06:38.100 --> 00:06:42.070
Plato recounts this night in his dialogue the Symposium.

72
00:06:42.070 --> 00:06:51.800
When the turn comes to Socrates, Socrates does something odd: instead of giving his own view, he relays what he was taught in his youth by a woman from Mantinea, Diotima.

73
00:06:51.800 --> 00:06:55.130
What Diotima describes is a ladder.

74
00:06:55.130 --> 00:07:00.060
A person begins by being drawn to a single beautiful body.

75
00:07:00.060 --> 00:07:09.470
Then, says Diotima, there is something this person must come to notice: the beauty in one body and the beauty in another body are kin, they are the same thing.

76
00:07:09.470 --> 00:07:16.000
Once you have seen this, you must loosen that violent passion for the single body, even come to think little of it.

77
00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:21.570
That is the second rung of the ladder: to love all beautiful bodies.

78
00:07:21.570 --> 00:07:30.180
Then the beauty of the soul is worth more than the beauty of the body; whoever sees a beautiful soul in an unlovely body should stop there and learn to love.

79
00:07:30.180 --> 00:07:35.270
Then the beauty of laws, of institutions, of human affairs.

80
00:07:35.270 --> 00:07:38.440
Then the beauty of knowledge.

81
00:07:38.440 --> 00:07:47.370
And at the very top, beauty itself, confined to no single instance, to no body and no person, never born, never dying, never diminishing.

82
00:07:47.370 --> 00:07:50.060
This is not a doctrine to be laughed at.

83
00:07:50.060 --> 00:08:01.230
It is extraordinarily consistent, and what it says is even breathtaking: what a human being really wants is to touch what is immortal, and falling in love with a body is the lowest rung of that very wanting.

84
00:08:01.230 --> 00:08:03.120
The ladder goes upward.

85
00:08:03.120 --> 00:08:09.330
But a ladder always does two things at once: it carries you up, and it leaves the rung below behind you.

86
00:08:09.330 --> 00:08:17.140
And it is exactly here, some 2400 years later, that one of the sharpest objections ever raised against this teaching arrived.

87
00:08:17.140 --> 00:08:26.390
The Plato scholar Gregory Vlastos, in a famous study published in 1973, asked this: what is happening to the people on that ladder?

88
00:08:26.390 --> 00:08:29.560
Vlastos's answer was a bitter one.

89
00:08:29.560 --> 00:08:35.770
In this picture we do not love persons as themselves; we love them as temporary carriers of excellences.

90
00:08:35.770 --> 00:08:45.340
What we love is the beauty, the intelligence, the virtue that shows up in the person before us; the person is merely the address where those qualities are currently located.

91
00:08:45.340 --> 00:08:48.990
And the address never becomes more than an address.

92
00:08:48.990 --> 00:08:56.480
In Vlastos's reading, there is no room there for a flawed, ordinary, incomplete human being to be loved entire.

93
00:08:56.480 --> 00:09:00.450
Do not treat this as abstract; you see it around you.

94
00:09:00.450 --> 00:09:06.500
The teacher who loves a student for their potential loses interest when the student fails to realise that potential.

95
00:09:06.500 --> 00:09:17.350
The person who describes their love by saying I fell in love with their mind feels, on the day the other does something unintelligent, not merely disappointment but something closer to a breach of contract.

96
00:09:17.350 --> 00:09:23.720
The family who describe their child through their achievements have no idea what to do when the failures start arriving.

97
00:09:23.720 --> 00:09:26.570
There is no ill will in any of this.

98
00:09:26.570 --> 00:09:33.900
There is the same structure in all of it: love has been fastened not to the person, but to a quality passing across the person.

99
00:09:33.900 --> 00:09:39.950
In the same world, in the same centuries, Aristotle came at the problem from another direction.

100
00:09:39.950 --> 00:09:47.920
He divided friendships into 3 kinds: those formed for usefulness, those formed for pleasure, and those formed for virtue.

101
00:09:47.920 --> 00:09:58.770
What he said about the first two still holds today; the one who loves for usefulness is in fact loving not the other person but the usefulness that comes from them, and when the usefulness is gone,

102
00:09:58.770 --> 00:10:01.300
the friendship goes with it.

103
00:10:01.300 --> 00:10:05.270
That much is true, but it does not solve our problem.

104
00:10:05.270 --> 00:10:11.000
Because the friendship Aristotle ranked highest also rests on a quality, namely virtue.

105
00:10:11.000 --> 00:10:19.290
If I love the person before me because they are a good person, the question of what happens when a better person shows up is still sitting on the table.

106
00:10:19.290 --> 00:10:24.380
The Greek world saw the dilemma with great clarity, but could not get out of the jaws.

107
00:10:24.380 --> 00:10:35.710
The answer that loosened those jaws came several centuries later, in a very different geography, and it came not from someone building a metaphysics but from someone who observed lovers one by one and took notes.

108
00:10:35.710 --> 00:10:46.240
Ibn Hazm was born in Córdoba in 994, the son of a family that had served in the upper ranks of the state, and he spent his youth watching the order of al-Andalus collapse.

109
00:10:46.240 --> 00:10:53.250
Their house was plundered, his father died, he himself went into exile repeatedly and spent time in prison.

110
00:10:53.250 --> 00:11:01.700
Around the year 1022, in Játiva, responding to a friend's insistence that he write a book on love, he sat down and wrote one.

111
00:11:01.700 --> 00:11:06.470
Its original title is Tawq al-Hamama, that is, The Ring of the Dove.

112
00:11:06.470 --> 00:11:15.560
That this book reached us at all is close to an accident: the only manuscript copy known in the world sits in the Netherlands, in the library of Leiden University.

113
00:11:15.560 --> 00:11:18.410
The Ring of the Dove is not a hymn to love.

114
00:11:18.410 --> 00:11:21.260
It is a notebook of observations.

115
00:11:21.260 --> 00:11:34.190
Ibn Hazm separates his chapters with the fastidiousness of a natural scientist: the nature of love, the signs of love, loving at a single glance, loving in a dream, loving on the strength of a description alone,

116
00:11:34.190 --> 00:11:39.920
messengers, those who keep the secret, separation, forgetting, death.

117
00:11:39.920 --> 00:11:51.250
Looking at that sequence, one feels less like reading a book about love than an atlas of a disease, which is largely what Ibn Hazm is doing: he recounts the lovers he has seen, one by one,

118
00:11:51.250 --> 00:11:53.940
most often concealing their names.

119
00:11:53.940 --> 00:11:59.350
And he says the thing that lands in the very middle of our question.

120
00:11:59.350 --> 00:12:03.160
Ibn Hazm argues that the cause of love is not beauty.

121
00:12:03.160 --> 00:12:10.970
His grounds are not theoretical but observational: if the cause were beauty, everyone would attach themselves to whoever is most beautiful.

122
00:12:10.970 --> 00:12:13.980
But that is not what actually happens.

123
00:12:13.980 --> 00:12:22.110
People look at someone far more beautiful, pass by, and give their hearts to another; and what is more, they cannot explain it to themselves either.

124
00:12:22.110 --> 00:12:28.160
For him, love is to be sought not in the sum of countable qualities but in a kinship between souls.

125
00:12:28.160 --> 00:12:33.250
But the most striking passage in the book on this matter is Ibn Hazm's own confession.

126
00:12:33.250 --> 00:12:37.380
He writes that in his youth he gave his heart to a fair-haired woman.

127
00:12:37.380 --> 00:12:45.190
And he adds: from that day on, however highly the beauty of dark-haired women was praised, he could never again incline toward it.

128
00:12:45.190 --> 00:12:48.840
Think that sentence through once more, slowly.

129
00:12:48.840 --> 00:12:52.170
Because here the whole business turns around.

130
00:12:52.170 --> 00:12:59.660
Here is what we assume: first we come to like certain qualities, then we find someone carrying those qualities, then we love them.

131
00:12:59.660 --> 00:13:03.310
The list comes first, the person second.

132
00:13:03.310 --> 00:13:07.440
What Ibn Hazm describes is the exact reverse.

133
00:13:07.440 --> 00:13:09.650
First he loved that person.

134
00:13:09.650 --> 00:13:15.060
Then the quality that person carried became, for him, the measure of beauty itself.

135
00:13:15.060 --> 00:13:19.190
The list is not the cause of the love but its consequence.

136
00:13:19.190 --> 00:13:27.000
We do not choose the person we love because of their properties; the person we love rewrites which properties we will find beautiful.

137
00:13:27.000 --> 00:13:30.970
You can test this in your own life.

138
00:13:30.970 --> 00:13:39.740
The way someone wrinkles their nose when they laugh, the meaningless little word they wedge into the middle of a sentence, the cracked sound of their voice in the morning.

139
00:13:39.740 --> 00:13:43.230
None of these were on any list of sought-after qualities.

140
00:13:43.230 --> 00:13:47.840
Nobody goes through life saying I would like to meet someone who wrinkles their nose.

141
00:13:47.840 --> 00:13:53.090
But once you have loved that person, that wrinkle becomes the most endearing thing in the world.

142
00:13:53.090 --> 00:14:02.500
Ibn Hazm noted this too: after a while, lovers become unable to see the flaw in the one they love as a flaw at all, and will even defend that flaw and praise it.

143
00:14:02.500 --> 00:14:07.270
The subtlety here is this: Ibn Hazm is not denying the properties.

144
00:14:07.270 --> 00:14:12.040
He is not saying that love is a blind collision entirely independent of them.

145
00:14:12.040 --> 00:14:16.010
What he is talking about is the direction of the arrow of causation.

146
00:14:16.010 --> 00:14:19.180
Properties are the door, not the house.

147
00:14:19.180 --> 00:14:29.230
A person enters a house through the door; but once they have begun to live in it, the reason they love that door is no longer the door itself, it is the life behind it.

148
00:14:29.230 --> 00:14:40.880
That a man who defended the Zahiri school in law, the school known for holding to the apparent meaning of the text and regarded as closed to interpretation, should go so far beneath the apparent when it came to love,

149
00:14:40.880 --> 00:14:43.410
looks like an interesting contradiction.

150
00:14:43.410 --> 00:14:49.620
Another name from the same century, Ibn Sina, in his Treatise on Love,

151
00:14:49.620 --> 00:14:57.910
took love out of the category of a condition belonging only to human beings and described it as the orientation of all existing things toward their own perfection.

152
00:14:57.910 --> 00:15:07.320
And the well-known line attributed to Yunus Emre points somewhere else again: to love what is created not on account of the qualities it possesses, but on account of the Creator.

153
00:15:07.320 --> 00:15:14.490
There is something common to all 3: the grounds of love are moved off the beloved's list of properties and onto something else.

154
00:15:14.490 --> 00:15:17.980
But Ibn Hazm's answer leaves a question open.

155
00:15:17.980 --> 00:15:20.830
Let us say we came in through the door.

156
00:15:20.830 --> 00:15:24.480
What happens over 30 years in the house?

157
00:15:24.480 --> 00:15:32.450
In 1563, when his friend Étienne de La Boétie died, Montaigne spent years looking for a way to describe that friendship.

158
00:15:32.450 --> 00:15:44.580
In his Essays, published in 1580, trying to explain why he had been so attached, he finally gave up and, saying that no reason was sufficient, wrote this: because it was he, because it was I.

159
00:15:44.580 --> 00:15:49.990
That sentence has been quoted for centuries, and is usually read as a poetic evasion.

160
00:15:49.990 --> 00:15:52.840
But there is a thesis inside it.

161
00:15:52.840 --> 00:16:01.450
Montaigne locates the grounds of love not in the qualities of the other, but in the meeting of 2 people as those particular persons.

162
00:16:01.450 --> 00:16:05.740
Contemporary philosophy has followed exactly this trail.

163
00:16:05.740 --> 00:16:16.110
Niko Kolodny's proposal, set out in 2003, is this: love does have a reason, but that reason is not a property; it is the relationship itself.

164
00:16:16.110 --> 00:16:24.880
I love this person because they are the one I have lived with for 10 years, the mother or the father of my child, the person who sat beside me when I was ill.

165
00:16:24.880 --> 00:16:29.170
This answers both the logic of the ladder and the duplicate at once.

166
00:16:29.170 --> 00:16:32.020
Because a shared history cannot be copied.

167
00:16:32.020 --> 00:16:37.910
Even if someone walked through the door carrying every one of their properties, our 10 years are not in them.

168
00:16:37.910 --> 00:16:43.000
And 10 years is not a quality; there can be no one who carries more of it.

169
00:16:43.000 --> 00:16:46.490
Harry Frankfurt went further still in 2004.

170
00:16:46.490 --> 00:16:52.860
For him, love is not a response given to the value of the other; it is the very thing that creates the value.

171
00:16:52.860 --> 00:17:00.830
His example is plain: parents do not love their children because their children are valuable; their children are valuable to them because they love them.

172
00:17:00.830 --> 00:17:05.120
Nobody examines the qualities of a newborn and then decides.

173
00:17:05.120 --> 00:17:09.250
There, love comes first and the reasons form afterwards.

174
00:17:09.250 --> 00:17:14.980
Frankfurt's claim is that adult love resembles this structure far more closely than it appears to.

175
00:17:14.980 --> 00:17:18.470
But is love a feeling or a decision that is kept up?

176
00:17:18.470 --> 00:17:23.880
In 1986 Amelie Rorty turned this question around from a fine angle.

177
00:17:23.880 --> 00:17:31.050
The famous standard in Shakespeare's sonnet 116 says that what alters in the face of alteration is not love.

178
00:17:31.050 --> 00:17:37.100
Rorty inverted it: the real defect lies in the love that does not change when the beloved changes.

179
00:17:37.100 --> 00:17:39.790
Because people do change.

180
00:17:39.790 --> 00:17:43.760
A person at 40 is not the same as the one at 20.

181
00:17:43.760 --> 00:17:52.690
To preserve, entirely unaltered, the love you felt for the 20 year old is not fidelity; it is ceasing to see the person in front of you.

182
00:17:52.690 --> 00:18:00.180
For Rorty, the real test of love is not remaining fixed, but being able to go on getting to know the other person again and again.

183
00:18:00.180 --> 00:18:05.430
Which brings us to what Erich Fromm said in 1956, in The Art of Loving.

184
00:18:05.430 --> 00:18:12.280
Fromm's real concern was to show that love is not a talent but an art, that is, something that has to be learned and practised.

185
00:18:12.280 --> 00:18:22.810
For him, love understood merely as a feeling is an unreliable foundation, because feelings come and go; love is at the same time a decision, a judgement, and a promise.

186
00:18:22.810 --> 00:18:30.140
Returning in the light of all this to the question we began with, it becomes clear that the question itself has been slightly badly built.

187
00:18:30.140 --> 00:18:33.150
Do you love them, or their qualities.

188
00:18:33.150 --> 00:18:36.800
That question takes the door and the house to be rivals.

189
00:18:36.800 --> 00:18:41.570
But the properties are not the reason for love, they are the way the meeting happened.

190
00:18:41.570 --> 00:18:48.260
Someone's laugh stops you, their intelligence brings you closer, their honesty makes it easier to stay.

191
00:18:48.260 --> 00:18:51.110
Without those you would have walked right past.

192
00:18:51.110 --> 00:19:01.320
But what accumulates over the years is no longer that list; it is a night spent at the hospital, a dog buried together, the silent peace made in the kitchen 2 hours after the argument.

193
00:19:01.320 --> 00:19:08.170
Nobody can count these as a property, because they do not belong to the person, they belong to what is between the 2 of you.

194
00:19:08.170 --> 00:19:13.740
So the sentence I love you for that quality of yours is not a lie; it is half of one.

195
00:19:13.740 --> 00:19:22.350
The whole of it is something more like this: I noticed you through that quality, then you and I built a history, and the reason I love you now is that history.

196
00:19:22.350 --> 00:19:29.520
In practice there is only one test of this, and it is taken not on the day the sentence is spoken but on the day the grounds disappear.

197
00:19:29.520 --> 00:19:38.610
The person who has heard they are loved for their intelligence finds out whether they are still loved at the end of a week in which they were exhausted and could not say a single clever thing.

198
00:19:38.610 --> 00:19:42.100
And Diotima's ladder was not wrong about everything.

199
00:19:42.100 --> 00:19:49.590
A human being really does try to become someone worth loving; nobody wants to give up their qualities and freeze where they stand.

200
00:19:49.590 --> 00:19:54.840
But what the ladder forgot was this: nobody wants to be loved as a specimen.

201
00:19:54.840 --> 00:20:04.890
Not as one copy of beauty, of intelligence, of goodness, but by their name, with their flaws, with that wrinkling nose, as someone for whom no substitute can be put.

202
00:20:04.890 --> 00:20:11.580
Perhaps that is love's real work in the face of the properties: to take a person out of a list and turn them into a name.

203
00:20:11.580 --> 00:20:15.230
Nöron Philosophy'de bugünkü bölümün sonuna geldik.

204
00:20:15.230 --> 00:20:19.040
Yeni bir hikâyede yeniden buluşmak üzere.
